# =============================================================================
# vllm.env — environment variables and rationale for running vLLM in this repo.
#
# This file is BOTH human documentation AND a template you can `source`:
#     source serve/vllm.env
#
# The launcher (serve/vllm.serve.sh) sets all of these itself; you only need this
# if you prefer to run `.venv-vllm/bin/vllm serve` directly by hand, or want the
# story of WHY each fix is required (useful context for operators and debugging).
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1) WSL2 pinned memory / CUDA UVA
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# vLLM detects WSL2 and, by default, DISABLES pinned (page-locked) host memory
# for its asynchronous GPU buffer allocator. That allocator relies on CUDA
# "Unified Virtual Addressing" (pinned host memory mapped into the GPU's VA),
# so disabling pinning makes vLLM throw at startup:
#
#     RuntimeError: UVA is not available          (buffer_utils.py :: UvaBuffer)
#
# WSL2 kernels >= 4.19.121 DO support pinned memory; it is merely off by default.
# (this box: 6.18.33.2-microsoft-standard-WSL2). Opt in explicitly:
#     VLLM_WSL2_ENABLE_PIN_MEMORY=1
VLLM_WSL2_ENABLE_PIN_MEMORY=1

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2) FlashInfer top-k/top-p sampler (the big one)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# By default vLLM uses FlashInfer kernels for the top-k/top-p sampling step.
# That kernel is JIT-compiled on first use via nvcc + ninja. This machine has:
#   - the NVIDIA DRIVER only (no system CUDA toolkit at /usr/local/cuda, no
#     bare-metal nvcc on PATH), AND
#   - the pip-installed `nvidia-cu13` nvcc (13.3.73) whose bundled CCCL headers
#     MISMATCH FlashInfer's bundled libcudacxx headers.
#
# The mismatch surfaces as a compile error:
#     #error "CUDA compiler and CUDA toolkit headers are incompatible"
#
# We therefore disable FlashInfer's sampler so vLLM uses its native Triton/Gumbel
# sampler instead. Statistically equivalent; avoids all fragile JIT compilation.
#     VLLM_USE_FLASHINFER_SAMPLER=0
VLLM_USE_FLASHINFER_SAMPLER=0

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3) CUDA_HOME -> pip-installed CUDA toolkit (nvidia/cu13)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# vLLM ships CUDA runtime/CUDA 13 components as pip wheels inside the venv. The
# pip `nvidia-cuda-nvcc` package provides a usable nvcc under:
#   .venv-vllm/lib/python3.10/site-packages/nvidia/cu13/bin/nvcc
# Pointing CUDA_HOME there lets any tool that probes $CUDA_HOME/bin/nvcc find a
# compiler (e.g. torch cpp_extension). Kept for completeness / future needs.
# NOTE: as of the current build this alone is NOT enough for FlashInfer JIT
# (see #2) because of the CCCL/libcudacxx header mismatch.
# CUDA_HOME="$PWD/.venv-vllm/lib/python3.10/site-packages/nvidia/cu13"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4) PATH (used by the .serve.sh launcher, not set here)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The venv's bin dir holds tools like `ninja` that JIT builds invoke via bare
# `subprocess.run(['ninja', ...])`. If you launch vLLM through the absolute
# venv path WITHOUT the venv bin on PATH, those tools aren't found:
#     FileNotFoundError: [Errno 2] No such file or directory: 'ninja'
# Activations or the launcher fix this. `source vllm.env` alone does NOT set it
# (it should only set env vars that are safe/explicit), so prefer the launcher.

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A) Model / memory sizing (this 8 GB laptop GPU)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PERMANENT serving model is Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct. On this 8 GB card only
# ~7.0 GiB is usable (driver reserves ~1 GiB). VRAM must hold: weights + KV
# cache + activations + CUDA-graph memory. Model choices:
#   - 3B bf16 (~6 GB weights): boots but leaves NEGATIVE KV-cache space -> vLLM
#     aborts ("No available memory for the cache blocks"; AV cache -0.45 GiB).
#     3B is NOT deployable here without quantization, so it is not a target.
#   - 1.5B: fits with ~2.7 GiB KV cache, but we standardize on 0.5B for generous
#     KV/sequence headroom under concurrent requests.
#   - A quantized 7B (AWQ) COULD fit via quantization; optional demonstration.
#
# GPU util 0.85 (not 0.90): 0.90 of 8 GiB (7.2) exceeds actually-free memory, so
# vLLM early-aborts:
#     Free memory ... is less than desired GPU memory utilization
MODEL=Qwen/Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct
GPU_UTIL=0.85
MAX_MODEL_LEN=2048
HOST=0.0.0.0
PORT=8000

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# B) Quick reference: launch the server
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   ./serve/vllm.serve.sh                  # foreground (Ctrl-C to stop)
#   nohup ./serve/vllm.serve.sh >/tmp/vllm.log & # background, logs to file
#   curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/health
#   curl http://127.0.0.1:8000/v1/models
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# C) Quick reference: shutdown the server
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   pkill -f "vllm serve"
#   pkill -f "VLLM::EngineCore"
#
#   Verify the shutdown (change port if applicable):
#   ss -tln | grep ':8000' || echo "port 8000 free"
#
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   D) Quick reference: vLLM API endpoints
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#   - GET  /health — is it ready?
#   - GET  /v1/models — what's loaded?
#   - POST /v1/chat/completions — the main workhorse (chat inference)
#   - POST /v1/completions — raw completion (no chat template), if ever needed
#   - GET  /metrics — for Prometheus/observability later
